  "body": "[Section 4.3.4](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/sections.html#the-nav-element) on the `nav` element has a note stating:\r\n\r\n> The footer element alone is sufficient [...] while a nav element can be used [...] it is usually unnecessary.\r\n\r\nIt then continues with the following example of a `footer` element containing various links to other pages such as _About_ and _Contact Us_:\r\n```html\r\n<footer>\r\n  [...]\r\n  <p><a href=\"about.html\">About</a> -\r\n     <a href=\"policy.html\">Privacy Policy</a> -\r\n     <a href=\"contact.html\">Contact Us</a></p>\r\n </footer>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe following [section 4.3.5](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/sections.html#the-aside-element) on the `aside` element has roughly the same markup in its example, but then uses a `nav` element in a seemingly contradicting manner:\r\n```html\r\n<footer>\r\n  <nav>\r\n   <a href=\"/archives\">Archives</a> —\r\n   <a href=\"/about\">About me</a> —\r\n   <a href=\"/copyright\">Copyright</a>\r\n  </nav>\r\n </footer>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf this is considered just as valid perhaps a note clarifying this inconsistency would be useful to readers. Otherwise I believe the two should follow the same example.",
